# The Vilij — redirects for the app pages that used to live on this domain.
#
# The marketing site moves from marketing.thevilij.co.uk to the apex
# thevilij.co.uk, and the Bubble app moves the other way, from the apex to
# app.thevilij.co.uk. So every app URL anyone has bookmarked, been emailed, or
# had saved by a password manager now points at this site. These send them on.
#
# Cloudflare Pages reads this file from the root of the build output, and Astro
# copies public/ there verbatim.
#
# WHY NO DESTINATION CARRIES A QUERY STRING. Pages forwards the incoming
# request's query string to the destination whenever the destination has none
# of its own — the `destination.search ? destination.search : search` branch in
# packages/pages-shared/asset-server/handler.ts in cloudflare/workers-sdk. That
# is load-bearing rather than incidental: /reset_pw and /email-confirmed are
# reached from links in emails and carry a one-time token in the query string.
# Putting a `?` on any destination below would replace the token rather than
# pass it on, and silently break password resets for anyone holding an old link.
#
# WHY EACH PAGE GETS TWO RULES. Bubble serves a page both at /page and at
# /page/<path> when the page takes path parameters, so the splat carries the
# rest of the path over. The splat also catches the trailing-slash form, which
# would otherwise 404 here.
#
# WHY THE EXACT RULES ARE ALL FIRST. The parser counts a rule as static only
# while it has seen no splat or placeholder rule before it: the first dynamic
# rule sets `canCreateStaticRule = false` and every rule after it is counted as
# dynamic, whether or not it contains a splat (parseRedirects.ts, same repo).
# The two budgets are 2,000 static and 100 dynamic, so interleaving these would
# spend 40 of the 100 dynamic slots instead of 20. Matching is unaffected —
# exact matches are looked up before any splat is tried, whatever the order —
# but keep the two blocks separate so the dynamic budget stays honest.
#
# THESE RULES BEAT STATIC ASSETS. Pages matches this file before it looks for a
# file to serve, so a rule here shadows a page of the same name. None of these
# names collide with a marketing route today; check that before adding more.
# (/404 is the app's own 404 page. It does not change what this site serves for
# an unknown path — Cloudflare serves 404.html internally rather than requesting
# /404 — so a branded 404 page here would still work.)
#
# 301 rather than 302: the move is permanent, and we want bookmarks and saved
# logins fixed for good.
#
# Both blocks are in the app's own page-list order, so they can be diffed
# against it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Exact paths
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The app's index page. Its other home was the bare domain, and that now belongs
# to the marketing homepage — someone with thevilij.co.uk bookmarked is meant to
# land on marketing, so there is deliberately no rule for /.
